Take rewind snapshots without stalling on the GPU; raise the emulation thread.

Measured in a 60 FPS session (heartbeat, run report): 100% of frames fitted
one field and the scripts ran at 29/s, yet the host presented 9,830 frames for
11,083 VBlanks and the player saw a low 1% frame rate. One cause is periodic
by construction. Rewind (on by default, "short" in the launcher) captures a
full machine snapshot every 15 VBlanks, and the VRAM part went through
gr_vram_transfer_out -> ensure_cpu(): flush the batches, pack, and a
synchronous glReadPixels of the whole 1024x512 VRAM. Into client memory that
makes the CPU wait until the GPU has finished everything queued - on the
emulation thread, four times a second. Whenever the GPU had most of a frame
in flight that is a missed field, 1 frame in 15: exactly what drags a 1% low.

  - gpu_render: optional vram_readback_begin/poll. The GL backend queues the
    same pack + read into a pixel-pack buffer, fences it and glFlush()es;
    poll() maps it once the fence has signalled. s_vram / s_gpu_dirty are left
    alone, so the next real ensure_cpu() behaves as before. glFenceSync and
    friends (GL 3.2 core; the context is 3.3) are loaded as optional, so a
    driver without them only loses the async path.
  - boot_state: boot_state_save_buffer_raw_deferred_vram writes the VRAM
    section with a zero payload and returns its offset (raw, little-endian,
    not with the netplay dirty-tracking mirror).
  - psx_rewind: do_capture starts the download at the same instant as the rest
    of the state, stores the snapshot straight away and fills its VRAM a frame
    or two later (polled at most once per frame). The snapshot is listed, and
    its thumbnail made from the downloaded bytes, only then; loading or opening
    the strip finishes a pending one first; a snapshot whose download failed
    is dropped, never loaded with a blank VRAM. Without the async path it
    captures synchronously as before.

Also: the emulation thread (main thread - emulation, rendering and pacing)
now runs at SDL_THREAD_PRIORITY_HIGH, i.e. THREAD_PRIORITY_HIGHEST on Windows,
so an ordinary busy process cannot preempt it for a scheduler quantum - the
other source of isolated spikes. The pacer sleeps on a high-resolution
waitable timer, so this does not become a busy core. PSX_THREAD_PRIORITY=0
opts out.

Heartbeat: rewind_async_captures / rewind_sync_captures.

No file here is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake; overlay shards are unaffected.

